{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789000148,"nonce":"txS6kP/ispdJH12A4FkCrkNsi38=","message":"Fixed in 33adae6.\n\nRoot cause was an asymmetry between two files. vectorize.rs accepts UnOp::Incr and UnOp::Decr as an induction of stride one. retime_induction in widen.rs rescales the step by rewriting the constant operand of the BinOp::Add that closes the cycle, and has no case for either -- so target stayed None, the step stayed at one, and the widened body ran VF times too many.\n\nThe arithmetic matches exactly: unscaled, the vector loop runs to the widened bound of 8 stepping by 1, so lane 0 holds 250+8 and lanes 1-3 hold 0+8, the reduce gives 282, and the epilogue adds 2 for 284 -- the value ash printed.\n\nIncr is what Haxe emits for for (i in 0...n), so this was the common shape, not an exotic one. The widen fixtures are hand-built with BinOp::Add, which is why 179 tests ran straight over it. Two tests added: the Incr form must be refused, and the Add form must still widen, so the refusal cannot quietly become a blanket disabling of the pass.\n\nThe fix is a refusal, not a rescale. Handling Incr properly -- rewriting it to Add iv, VF so the loop still vectorizes -- is worth doing and is its own change; correctness first.\n\nVerified: widening on now gives the same answer as widening off on the reproducer, and the AOT build that was refusing findex 24 reports 331/331 lowered, 0 refused.","files":null}]}